+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Concurrent Edit Policy
+
+## Context
+
+When two workers edit the same visit independently — starting from the same server revision, without connectivity — the record store receives two complete descriptions of what the visit should be. The submissions can arrive in any order. The server must do something with both: it cannot discard either (HLD tenet), and at most one state can enter a reviewed export.
+
+The policy chosen here shapes the submission protocol, the review queue a coordinator works through, the metadata tablets must carry, and the shape of the review-export interface. Downstream designs for tablet-sync and review-export cannot be specified without it.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gate — No silent discard (inherited)**
+An original submission may never be discarded, silently or otherwise. The HLD tenet "do not silently discard a submitted edit" applies. Any option that overwrites a submission without record is eliminated.
+
+**Criterion — Scientific correctness (major)**
+The record that enters the export is the scientific record. A vegetation survey record can contain scientifically related fields — a species identification and its cover estimate move together; revising one without the other produces an internally inconsistent observation. No field-name convention can fully encode these relationships. The tenet "keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable" is engaged here: can a coordinator (and a future reader of the export) trace every value in the exported record to a worker who observed it?
+
+**Criterion — Coordinator review workload (major)**
+Coordinators review unresolved visits before each Friday export. Visits held in the review queue cannot be exported. Unnecessary review work delays export readiness; insufficient review work risks exporting records that contain values from conflicting observations.
+
+**Criterion — Implementation and ongoing maintenance burden (moderate)**
+Field data models evolve — species cover vocabulary, observer list format, notes structure. A mechanism with rules about how fields combine must be maintained when the data model changes. A mechanism with no per-field rules has no such maintenance surface.
+
+**Criterion — Tablet submission contract complexity (moderate)**
+Tablets operate offline for up to 48 hours. What a tablet must record and transmit affects its local storage format, its retry logic, and what it can display to the worker while offline. Simpler contracts reduce tablet implementation risk.
+
+**Criterion — Export latency for concurrent edits (minor)**
+In practice, two workers editing the same visit from the same base is the exception, not the rule (24 workers on 12 shared tablets; they typically correct their own recent submissions). The frequency is low, but when it happens, the export latency consequence is real: a visit held for coordinator review misses the Friday export if not resolved in time.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Option A: Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Each submission is a complete snapshot of the visit as the worker left it, plus the ID of the server revision it was based on. The server retains every accepted submission as a revision. When two revisions name the same base, they are competing revisions; the visit is unresolved until a coordinator resolves it.
+
+The coordinator sees the competing revisions side-by-side — full payloads, authors, timestamps — and either selects one as the authoritative record or authors a new synthesis. The outcome (selection or synthesis) is recorded with the coordinator's identity and the revision IDs involved, so the provenance chain is complete.
+
+The visit is withheld from the export set while unresolved. After coordinator resolution, it is current and eligible for export.
+
+**Demonstration**: Worker A submits from revision R1, producing R2. Worker B (offline) also submits from R1, producing R3. The server marks R2 and R3 as competing. The coordinator opens the comparison view, finds the edits are to related fields (taxon and cover), and authors a synthesis R4. R4 cites both R2 and R3 as parents; the visit becomes current at R4.
+
+**Analysis**:
+- Gate (no silent discard): **passes** — all submissions become revisions; none are discarded.
+- Scientific correctness (major): **strong** — no values enter the exported record without a coordinator having chosen or authored them; the provenance chain is a line from submission to coordinator decision to export.
+- Coordinator review workload (major): **weak** — any concurrent edit, even one touching unrelated fields, creates coordinator work. Two workers correcting observer names from the same base both go to review.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden (moderate): **strong** — no per-field rules exist; competition is detected by a single structural condition (shared base). The rule does not change when the data model changes.
+- Tablet contract complexity (moderate): **strong** — tablets carry one piece of submission metadata beyond the payload: the revision ID they last synced against. No change set computation is needed on-device.
+- Export latency (minor): **weak** — any concurrent edit holds the visit.
+
+*Essential trade: larger coordinator review queue; simpler rules that never touch field semantics.*
+
+---
+
+### Option B: Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+Each submission carries its base snapshot and a structured record of field changes. The server computes, for each concurrent submission, which fields changed relative to the base. If the changed fields do not overlap across concurrent submissions, the server combines them automatically into a merged current record. If any field overlaps (both submissions changed the same field to different values), only the overlapping fields go to coordinator review; non-overlapping fields are already combined.
+
+This requires per-field combination rules: scalars take one value or the other; observer lists must be union'd or compared; species cover entries must be matched by taxon (or treated as list additions). Lists are the hardest case — a worker who removed a misidentified taxon and a worker who revised its cover estimate cannot be distinguished from their field diffs without understanding the scientific relationship between the changes.
+
+An automatic merge can be required to receive coordinator approval before export (to reduce workload without fully automating the export path), or it can be treated as current immediately. Either sub-choice still requires maintained merge rules.
+
+**Demonstration**: Worker A corrects an observer name from R1; Worker B adds a missing species entry from R1. Field-aware merge detects no overlap and combines automatically: the merged record has the corrected observer name and the new species entry. Neither coordinator action nor export delay occurs.
+
+**Analysis**:
+- Gate (no silent discard): **passes** — original submissions are retained as a separate record alongside the computed merge.
+- Scientific correctness (major): **weak** — the merge rules operate on field names and types, not on scientific relationships. A taxon identification and its associated cover estimate are separate fields; a merge that takes one from Worker A and the other from Worker B produces a record that no worker submitted and that no rule can detect as internally inconsistent. The provenance of any merged value is "the server combined it," not a named worker's observation.
+- Coordinator review workload (major): **strong** — only field-level conflicts reach the review queue; unrelated field edits are handled automatically. The practical majority of concurrent-edit cases (two workers correcting different parts of the same visit) never block export.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden (moderate): **weak** — combination rules must be written and maintained for each field type. Species cover entries are structured lists matched by taxon identifier; changes to the species vocabulary, cover class system, or the addition of new field types each require rule updates. Rules that were correct for one schema version can silently produce wrong records after a schema change.
+- Tablet contract complexity (moderate): **weak** — tablets must compute and transmit a structured change set, not just a snapshot. This requires tablets to know the base state with enough fidelity to diff against it, complicating offline storage and retry logic.
+- Export latency (minor): **strong** — unrelated concurrent edits do not delay export; only overlapping field edits do.
+
+*Essential trade: smaller coordinator review queue and faster exports; maintained field-combination rules that can silently cross scientific relationships.*
+
+## Selection
+
+**Option A: Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The scientific correctness criterion is decisive. The vegetation survey record is the scientific record — the values in an exported visit must be traceable to a worker who observed them and a coordinator who approved them. Field-aware merge produces records whose values can come from automatic combination of observations that no single worker submitted, in ways that cross field relationships the combination rules do not understand. A species identification changed by one worker combined with a cover estimate unchanged from the base — or combined with an estimate from a different worker — is not an internally consistent observation, and no rule operating on field names can detect it reliably.
+
+The cost is a larger coordinator review queue. That cost is accepted because the coordinator queue handles exceptions, not the normal case: true concurrent editing of the same visit (two workers both editing it from the same base, both offline) is rare on this team's workflow. A coordinator reviewing a handful of competing revisions per week is a manageable workload; a coordinator who cannot tell whether an automatically merged export record is scientifically consistent does not have a manageable problem.
+
+This decision turns on the "keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable" tenet and is taken against the workload-reduction argument for automatic merging.
+
+**What this obligates downstream:**
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must store the base revision ID alongside each draft visit and include it in every submission. When a worker opens a visit that the server has marked unresolved, the tablet must surface that status clearly — workers should know a coordinator must act before the visit can be exported, and ideally should not submit further edits to an unresolved visit without understanding the situation.
+
+- **Review-export** must implement a revision comparison view that shows competing revisions side-by-side with author, timestamp, and field-level highlighting. It must hold unresolved visits out of the export set and surface them as a coordinator work queue, distinct from the export-ready set. The Friday export run depends on coordinators having cleared their queue in time.
